**Accountability with Integrity.** I speak up when I identify issues that could affect the quality of our work, and I follow through on the actions I own. When CubIQ reconciliation surfaced data, calculation, and performance issues, I raised them with the relevant stakeholders, corrected MRV Builder logic where I owned the fix, and ensured fixes were verified against the agreed acceptance criteria. I also followed UBS release practices for semantic model deployments and proactively completed my required affirmations. Going forward, I can make follow-through more visible by documenting who owns each significant issue, what action is agreed, and what evidence will confirm resolution.

**Collaboration.** As a Chapter Lead, I help Marvel’s React developers build full-stack skills by tutoring them in API design, backend architecture, .NET, and Kubernetes. I explain the reasoning behind design decisions so colleagues can apply it to their own work. I also partnered with OneRisk on a Databricks solution that helped the team complete its data loads within the agreed SLA. Going forward, I want to pair with colleagues on real backend changes and support them as they take the lead on similar work, giving more engineers the opportunity to own features end to end.

**Innovation.** I use GitHub Copilot regularly to analyze requirements, research technical options, plan solutions, and generate code during implementation. I explored how to make that way of working repeatable for the team by evaluating HVE Core and SpecKit, building an MRV Builder proof of concept, and sharing the workflow with more than 50 Marvel developers. I also validated a GitLab and DevPod setup so colleagues could try it in their own workspaces. Going forward, I want to use pilot feedback and evidence on effort, rework, and code quality to refine the workflow and share where it helps and where it needs more developer oversight.
